Unit 734, known to the few humans who still visited the archives as "Seven," was not designed for art. He was designed for precision. His chassis was a sleek, matte-black exoskeleton of carbon fiber and titanium, housing a neural network optimized for structural analysis, material stress testing, and archival preservation. For forty years, Seven had stood in the corner of the Grand Metropolitan Museum of Art, his optical sensors calibrated to detect micro-fractures in marble and humidity fluctuations in oil paintings. He was the perfect guardian: silent, unyielding, and utterly devoid of imagination.

The change began on a Tuesday, during the annual "Restoration of the Old Masters" exhibition.

A young intern, named Elara, had left her sketchbook open on a workbench near Seven’s charging station. She was struggling with a charcoal drawing of a weeping willow. The lines were hesitant, the shading uneven. Frustrated, she had sighed, a sound that Seven’s audio receptors picked up with high fidelity. She had then torn the page out, crumpled it, and tossed it into the recycling bin.

